# {{name}} server

A small Node server with no dependencies (Node 20 or newer). What it
answers is in `API.md`.

```sh
cp .env.example .env    # optional: the defaults work on a development machine
npm start               # listens on 127.0.0.1:{{port}}
npm test                # node --test
curl http://127.0.0.1:{{port}}/v1/health
```

A phone on USB reaches it through `adb reverse tcp:{{port}} tcp:{{port}}`.

## Where things are

| File | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `src/config.js` | Settings from the environment and `.env` |
| `src/app.js` | The pipeline (token, device id, rate limit, error shape, log line) and the routes |
| `src/server.js` | Starts it |
| `test/app.test.js` | The server through real HTTP on a free port |

## Rules worth keeping

- Secrets live in `.env` only, which git ignores. `.env.example` names the
  settings and holds no real value.
- The log line is method, path, status and time: never a header, a body
  or a token.
- The hello count is in memory. Put a real resource in a database and
  give the tests an in-memory stand-in.
